  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​670 — October 21, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191776/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Postgres Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191738/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/g1qnzow76mrbuxqpi5rj.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191738/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.enterprisedb.com\">Postgres 19: What Changed and What to Test Now</a></span> — With RC1 out and GA due in under a month, Tom Kincaid walks through what survived the reverts and what to try before upgrading, including <code>REPACK (CONCURRENTLY)</code>, <code>pg_plan_advice</code>, parallel autovacuum, and logical replication sequence synchronization, each with a 'what to test' checklist.</p>\n<p>Tom Kincaid (EDB) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>⭐ <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191739/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres 19 RC1 was released last week</a>, with an RC2 due November 12 and the final release expected on November 19.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191737/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/6d11cce3.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"180\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191737/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"serpapi.com\">100+ Search Engines. One Simple API</a></span> — Add live web search to your backend without managing proxies or parsers. SerpApi delivers structured results from Google and other search engines as clean JSON.</p>\n<p>SerpApi <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #997 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191741/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"ardentperf.com\"><code>recovery_prefetch</code> Considered Harmful and Causing Outages</a></span> — Jeremy shows how <code>recovery_prefetch</code>, enabled by default since Postgres 15, can overwhelm standby storage after a write burst, causing hours of lag. With synchronous replication, a dropped connection then blocked primary writes for 35 minutes in <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191742/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">his reproduction.</a></p>\n<p>Jeremy Schneider </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>🩹 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191743/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">A patch to avoid redundant prefetches after full-page images</a> is under review, which tackles part of the problem.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>🇺🇸 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191744/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres Conference 2027</a> takes place April 21–23, 2027 in San Jose, California. If you want to speak, its <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191745/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">CFP is open</a> until January 31.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191746/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Who owns an abandoned extension?</a> PGXN's David Wheeler asks <code>pgsql-hackers</code> what the policy should be, after receiving takeover requests and privilege escalation reports for unmaintained extensions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇧🇪 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191747/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">FOSDEM PGDay 2027</a> expands to two days, January 28–29, in Brussels. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191748/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">The CFP is open</a> until December 1.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇹🇭 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191749/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">FOSSASIA PGDay 2027</a> takes place on March 9, 2027 in Bangkok. Their <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191750/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">CFP is open</a> until November 30.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191751/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Neon Realtime</a> is now in public beta. You can subscribe to any deterministic SQL query and the result stays live as the data changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191752/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">84% of surveyed Ruby on Rails developers run Postgres in production</a> as per the results of the 2026 Rails Community Survey.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191753/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.depesz.com\">How <code>hstore</code> Can Beat <code>jsonb</code> for Audit Logs</a></span> — depesz saw <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191754/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">a post</a> claiming that logging only changed columns from an audit trigger shrank the log 5x but slowed updates. But his own test, using <code>hstore</code> instead of <code>jsonb</code>, made diff logging the fastest variant.</p>\n<p>Hubert 'depesz' Lubaczewski </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191756/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.pgedge.com\">Why is Postgres's <code>numeric</code> Type So Slow?</a></span> — A deep dive into <code>numeric</code>'s internals explaining why it's slow compared to double precision or DuckDB's integer-backed <code>DECIMAL</code>, and why <em>\"just use int128\"</em> doesn't fix it.</p>\n<p>Andrei Lepikhov (pgEdge) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191757/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"stackgres.io\">Citus Hits 10M QPS on Half <em>Neki</em>'s Data Nodes</a></span> — A month after PlanetScale's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191777/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">118M QPS Neki post</a>, Álvaro ran the same shape of workload on Citus with StackGres's new query routers and hit 10M QPS on 24 workers rather than Neki's 50 shards.</p>\n<p>Álvaro Hernández (StackGres) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>🎧 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191759/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres FM had Álvaro on the same day</a> for an hour on Citus, StackGres and the thinking behind the benchmark.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191760/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Why Materialize Dropped <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> for 3x Write Throughput</a> – Hundreds of thousands of predicate locks sank their Postgres-backed metadata store, so they rebuilt it on <code>READ COMMITTED</code> and <em>proved</em> it correct in Lean. <cite>Petros Angelatos</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191762/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">From Alert to Online Repack: A Playbook for Postgres Table Bloat</a> – Build a bloat 'watchdog' with <code>pg_cron</code> and <code>pgstattuple</code>, then use <code>pg_repack</code> to take a 13GB table down to 3.4GB without taking it offline. <cite>Haider Z (Microsoft)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191761/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Reducing Storage Initialization Impact in Amazon RDS After Snapshot Restore</a> – Why queries on a freshly restored RDS Postgres can run 10–15x slower at first, and how to prewarm it. <cite>Modappady, Pattabiraman and Thakur (AWS)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191763/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">UUIDs Stored as <code>text</code>: What It Costs in Postgres 18</a> <cite>Hollis Varden</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>Today: Let a coding agent fork, break, and fix a real Postgres database. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191764/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Free live workshop</a>, recording included.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES AND CODE:</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191765/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/sv56ilc9yxghvv6sdnem.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191765/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"documentdb.io\">DocumentDB 1.0: A MongoDB-Compatible Document Database on Postgres</a></span> — First open sourced by Microsoft last year but now a Linux Foundation project, DocumentDB is a set of extensions (plus a gateway) that speak the MongoDB wire protocol and store BSON natively. This first stable release comes with packages for Postgres 17 and 18.</p>\n<p>DocumentDB </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191766/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"pgx.dev\">PGX: Run Thousands of Throwaway Databases in One Process</a></span> — From the team behind <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191767/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">pgbot</a>, and built upon a Rust reimplementation of Postgres, PGX hosts large numbers of isolated databases in one process, each cloned from a template in milliseconds, for agent sandboxes and per-PR tests. Beta, AGPL-licensed, with <code>fsync</code> off, so for dev use only. (<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191768/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">GitHub repo</a>.)</p>\n<p>PGRun </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191769/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"pgbackrest.org\">pgBackRest 2.60: Restores Now Stop on Invalid Page Checksums</a></span> — The popular backup and restore tool now stops a restore with an error if any restored file has an invalid page checksum. Worth knowing before your next restore! (<code>checksum-page-error=n</code> can change it to a warning only.)</p>\n<p>pgBackRest Community </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191770/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_hint_plan 1.9.0</a> – The long-standing query hinting extension, now built (only) for Postgres 19 using its new path generation strategies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191771/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PostgREST 16.5</a> – Popular REST API server layer for Postgres. Adds a client-side mitigation for a recent Postgres CVE.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>⚠️ <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191772/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pgmq 1.13.1</a> – The Postgres-native message queue. Fixes two privilege issues, so upgrade if you use it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191774/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_ivm 1.17</a> – Incrementally maintained materialized views, which can now survive <code>pg_upgrade</code> if you use <code>pg_ivm_dump_metadata --pg-upgrade</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🌐 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/191775/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PostGIS 3.7.0 RC3</a> – The geospatial data extension targets Postgres 19 RC1 – I'd expect a final 3.7 around the same time as Postgres 19 itself.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}